Remote access
administrator-05-remote-access-and-the-machine
The page has six views, chosen with the keys 1 to 6. The doors view shows what REMOTE.API and REMOTE.SSH say with no keyword, which is what the machine reports after reading sd.conf and the firewall rules back. A changes the API (on, local or off) and S changes ssh (on or off), each chosen from a list that starts on off. A's form also asks whether to restart SD now (later is the default), because SD opens the socket only when it starts, so any change of the listener needs a restart (local listens on 127.0.0.1 only, on on every address), and a restart ends every session, this one too; answered later, the change waits for SD's next start. Nothing is asked on the terminal. S takes effect at once. Turning either on lets other computers reach this one, so its box wants the word open typed (in either case). The routes view shows each user account with the doors it has and how it can be reached; the switches themselves are on the gates page.
The servers view lists the SDNet servers of the netservers file: the name, the address, the remote account and mode, the pin shortened, and the local accounts that may use the server. X deletes the one under the cursor after a box. N asks for every field, checks them as SET.SERVER does, and gives the terminal to the verb, whose CONNECT step shows the key the server presents and asks whether to trust it. A local account also needs the NET gate.
The connected view lists every session by the door it came through, and the TLS view shows this machine's certificate and the hash of its public key, which another SD server pins. The remote admin view shows what REMOTE.SDSYS says with no keyword: whether the remote administration account SDRADMIN may sign in over ssh, one line for each wall. O opens it after a box that wants the word open typed (in either case), giving the terminal to REMOTE.SDSYS ON, which makes the account and asks its password the first time; F shuts it again and logs SDRADMIN out. Each command shows what it does and runs only when you answer Run; Cancel is what Enter chooses. F5 reads everything again.
Under the remote admin view's report are two fields, Local ssh users and Local API users: the Windows users who may reach SDSYS from this computer, by ssh -t name@localhost sd-sdsys or by a program over the API at 127.0.0.1, each then with the SD password of sdsys. Both lists are empty as delivered, and an empty list changes nothing. Up and Down choose a list. U adds a name: the box says what listing it means and wants the name typed again, because that user can then become the administrator. X removes a name chosen from the list and C clears the list. The page runs local.sdsys for each change, and its helper checks every name again: an existing local Windows user, enabled, not a built-in account, not SDSYS, in sdusers.
